.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt USERADD 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm useradd
.Nd add a user
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl D
.Op Fl b Ar base-dir
.Op Fl e Ar expiry
.Op Fl g Ar gid | name | Li =uid
.Op Fl k Ar skel-dir
.Op Fl L Ar class
.Op Fl M Ar homeperm
.Op Fl r Ar low..high
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Nm
.Op Fl mov
.Op Fl b Ar base-dir
.Op Fl c Ar comment
.Op Fl d Ar home-dir
.Op Fl e Ar expiry
.Op Fl G Ar group Ns Op , Ns Ar group ...
.Op Fl g Ar gid | name | Li =uid
.Op Fl k Ar skel-dir
.Op Fl L Ar class
.Op Fl M Ar homeperm
.Op Fl p Ar password
.Op Fl r Ar low..high
.Op Fl s Ar shell
.Op Fl u Ar uid
.Ar user
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility adds
.Ar user
to the account database, in the shape of the
.Nx
and
.Ox
command.
It is
.Xr pw 8
under another name, and changes the database the same way: under its
lock, checked, and installed as
.Xr pwd_mkdb 8
would.
It must be run by the superuser.
.Pp
Options not given take their defaults from
.Xr usermgmt.conf 5 .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-g gid|name|=uid"
.It Fl b Ar base-dir
The directory the home is made in, as
.Ar base-dir Ns / Ns Ar user ,
when
.Fl d
is not given.
.It Fl c Ar comment
The comment (GECOS) field, usually the full name.
.It Fl D
Without other options, print the defaults.
With them, change the defaults in
.Xr usermgmt.conf 5
and print the result.
.It Fl d Ar home-dir
The home directory.
.It Fl e Ar expiry
When the account expires: seconds since the Epoch, a date as
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD ,
.Ar dd mmm yyyy
or
.Ar mmm dd yyyy ,
or
.No + Ns Ar n Ns Op Cm hdwmy
from now.
0 means never.
.It Fl G Ar group Ns Op , Ns Ar group ...
Supplementary groups, by name or ID.
The option may be repeated.
.It Fl g Ar gid | name | Li =uid
The primary group.
.Li =uid ,
the default, makes a new group named like the user, with the user ID
as its group ID when that is free; the user ID is then chosen free as
both.
.It Fl k Ar skel-dir
The skeleton directory copied into a new home.
A file named
.Pa dot.foo
there becomes
.Pa .foo .
.It Fl L Ar class
The
.Xr login.conf 5
class.
.It Fl M Ar homeperm
The mode of the new home directory, in octal.
.It Fl m
Create the home directory, owned by the user, from the skeleton.
An existing directory is kept and only skeleton files it lacks are
added.
.It Fl o
Allow a user ID already in use.
.It Fl p Ar password
The password, already hashed by
.Xr crypt 3 .
Without it the account has no usable password
.Pq Ql *
until
.Xr passwd 1
sets one.
.It Fl r Ar low..high
The range new user and group IDs are taken from.
.It Fl s Ar shell
The login shell.
.It Fl u Ar uid
The user ID; the lowest free one in the range by default.
.It Fl v
Say what was done.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/usermgmt.conf -compact
.It Pa /etc/master.passwd
the account database
.It Pa /etc/group
the groups
.It Pa /etc/usermgmt.conf
the defaults
.It Pa /etc/skel
the default skeleton
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
As
.Xr pw 8 :
0 on success, a
.Xr sysexits 3
value on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add a user with a home directory, also in the
.Ql wheel
group:
.Dl # useradd -m -G wheel -c \(dqAlice Liddell\(dq alice
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr passwd 1 ,
.Xr group 5 ,
.Xr passwd 5 ,
.Xr usermgmt.conf 5 ,
.Xr adduser 8 ,
.Xr groupadd 8 ,
.Xr pw 8 ,
.Xr userdel 8 ,
.Xr usermod 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
utility appeared in
.Nx 1.5
and
.Ox 2.7 .
This implementation was written for OxideBSD.
OxideBSD keeps no inactive time, so
.Fl f
is not offered.
